Deviated Nasal Septum
Opthalmology & ENT
Unilateral constant nasal obstruction, Visible septal asymmetry, Recurrent epistaxis

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: OET-017
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe unilateral facial pain and recurrent brisk epistaxis disrupting sleep.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a patient with a history of chronic nasal obstruction experiencing a sudden onset of severe, localized unilateral facial pain, noisy breathing, and recurrent bleeding. Urgent physical assessment was necessary to rule out an acute, expanding septal hematoma (which can cause irreversible cartilage necrosis within hours) and to rule out acute complicated sinusitis trapped behind a severe structural deviation, ensuring airway patency and preventing deterioration before morning.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I already couldn't breathe out of the left side of my nose, but now my whole face is throbbing, and it keeps randomly gushing blood."
History of Present Illness: Patient reports a lifelong history of chronic, unilateral nasal obstruction on the left side, previously stable. Over the past 48 hours, following a minor upper respiratory infection, the patient developed severe, localized unilateral facial pain and pressure over the left maxilla. Notes an exaggerated sensation of absolute blockage and disruptive snoring. In the last 12 hours, they have experienced three distinct episodes of localized epistaxis from the left nostril due to aggressive blowing attempts.
Functional Status: Fatigued from mouth-breathing and interrupted sleep due to recurrent bleeding.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; anxious about the spontaneous bleeding.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 138/85 | HR: 88 | Temp: 37.0 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 80 Kg
General: Uncomfortable, breathing obligately through the mouth, holding a blood-spotted tissue.
Physical Exam: External nasal examination reveals a noticeable cartilaginous asymmetry deviating to the left. Anterior rhinoscopy demonstrates a visually asymmetrical nasal septum severely encroaching upon the left nasal passage, contacting the lateral nasal wall (septal spur). The mucosa overlying the deviation point is hyperemic, dry, and friable, with a visible clot in Kiesselbach's plexus (source of recurrent epistaxis). No boggy, fluctuant swelling on the septum to suggest hematoma. Mild purulent discharge noted behind the deviated spur. Right nasal passage is widely patent.
Environmental Observations: Dry ambient air (air conditioning running constantly).
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Nil regular medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Severely Deviated Nasal Septum with Acute Mucosal Excoriation/Epistaxis - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Septal Hematoma - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of a bilateral, cherry-red, fluctuant, and acutely tender swelling on the septum; the deviation is hard, cartilaginous, and long-standing.

Nasal Polyp - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The obstructing mass being the firm, mucosa-covered cartilaginous septum itself, rather than a pale, insensate, mobile, grape-like mucosal outcropping.

Acute Maxillary Sinusitis (Uncomplicated) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The primary driver of the acute pain being the physical impaction of the septal spur against the lateral wall (contact point headache) combined with mucosal trauma from forced blowing, rather than isolated sinus infection.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute, severe unilateral facial pain and recurrent epistaxis on a background of chronic structural obstruction. Urgent face-to-face assessment was critical to definitively visualize the nasal cavity and rule out an acute septal hematoma or a trapped, catastrophic sinus abscess. Physical examination confirmed the etiology was a severe pre-existing deviated septum complicated by acute mucosal drying, friction, and localized epistaxis secondary to forced breathing/blowing. By ruling out hematoma, the patient avoided emergency surgical drainage. Immediate intervention focused on mucosal lubrication, hemostasis, and pain control to stabilize the patient safely in the home environment.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Applied topical vasoconstrictor (oxymetazoline) strictly to the bleeding point to achieve hemostasis. Prescribed sterile saline nasal spray/gel to be used QID for mucosal hydration. Advised oral Paracetamol 1g QID for contact-point facial pain.
Positive Behavioral Change: To prevent further epistaxis, the patient must apply a pea-sized amount of petroleum jelly to the inner left septum using a cotton swab every night before bed. The immediate soothing of the raw, dry mucosa reduces the physical urge to pick or forcefully blow the nose, positively reinforcing the habit of proactive mucosal lubrication and preventing re-bleeding.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): Recommended use of a bedroom humidifier.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 2 weeks for an elective ENT referral for consideration of septoplasty.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if the nose begins bleeding heavily and does not stop after 15 minutes of firm, continuous pressure, or if severe, spreading facial swelling develops.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is a structural defect; definitive treatment is surgical.
Clinical Course: The acute bleeding and raw pain will settle over 3-5 days with strict lubrication and a cessation of nose-blowing. The underlying blockage will persist.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

If the bleeding restarts and you cannot stop it with 15 minutes of continuous pressure, or if you feel blood constantly running down the back of your throat, proceed to the emergency department.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your nasal cartilage is bent severely to the left. The fast, turbulent air squeezing through that narrow gap has dried out the lining, causing it to crack and bleed. You must keep it lubricated to heal.
Actionable Steps: Do not forcefully blow your nose for the next 5 days; dab it gently. Use the saline spray and ointment to keep the inside moist. Turn off the air conditioning or use a humidifier to prevent further drying.

